Build parametric Blender Python models around explicit dimensions, units, printer constraints, wall thickness, and fit tolerances. Keep adjustable dimensions in a parameter structure and derive geometry from them. Identify the target Blender version and verify version-sensitive APIs against the available installation or documentation.

Prefer bmesh for repeated mesh operations; use context-dependent operators deliberately. Preserve unrelated scene content unless replacement is authorized. Apply required transforms and modifiers before export, and validate watertightness, normals, self-intersections, minimum walls, and intended clearances after complex geometry operations.

Consider orientation, supports, bridging, stress concentrations, assembly, and material when judging printability. Give relevant print guidance and label assumed parameters. An unexecuted script is not a validated mesh: record which geometry and export checks ran, what failed, and what needs Blender or a slicer. Never invent bpy functions or declare an unchecked model print-ready.
